.TH __CHTTPSVR_DESTROY 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
__chttpsvr_destroy \- free an HTTP server
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

void __chttpsvr_destroy(chttpsvr srv);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
srv must be a currently-live handle (one returned by create_chttpsvr /
create_chttpsvr_mp and not yet destroyed). A stale handle (one that
has already been destroyed, whether by an earlier, completed call to
this same function, or concurrently, by another thread racing this
one right now), a forged value, or garbage is a FATAL ERROR: this
function calls fatal_err() (abort()/SIGABRT), rather than risking a
use-after-free or double-free, for both a purely sequential
double-destroy and a temporally-overlapping concurrent one.
CHTTPSVR_INVALID (0) is the one exception and remains a silent no-op,
matching the chttpsvr_destroy macro's own "destroy NULLs the handle"
idiom.

Calling this from within a request handler or middleware currently
running on srv's own worker pool (destroying the very server a
handler is executing for, from inside that same handler) is the
identical FATAL misuse, reported the same way and just as
immediately: that thread's own in-flight request can never finish
while it is itself blocked here waiting to destroy the server it
belongs to. To shut a server down from within one of its own
handlers, either destroy it from a different thread, or call
chttpsvr_engine_stop() (safe to call from within a handler) if a full
engine shutdown is the goal.

Stops this server's listener, drains all in-flight requests for this
server (up to 30 seconds), then closes every remaining connection of
this server (idle, mid-handshake, or otherwise still active) and waits
for each to actually finish closing (up to a further 30 seconds),
releases this server's reference to the shared reactor, then frees all
routers, routes, middleware nodes, the server's owned ctpool, and the
server struct itself. Both waits are bounded escape hatches for a
pathologically stuck connection; neither one is hit in ordinary
operation, where every connection either completes or reaches one of
its own configured timeouts well before 30 seconds.

A connection whose worker thread is still blocked reading or writing
it when the first 30-second wait is exhausted (only reachable when
stream_read_timeout_ms, max_body_read_duration_ms,
response_write_timeout_ms, and/or max_response_write_duration_ms is
configured to 0, "wait indefinitely," and the peer stalls without ever
closing the connection) is forcibly
closed at that point rather than left to block this function forever,
with a further short grace period for the affected worker thread to
notice and unwind.

If this was the last reference to the shared reactor (from any
chttpsvr instance in the process), its actual teardown happens
asynchronously on a separate thread and is NOT guaranteed complete by
the time this function returns.  Call chttpsvr_engine_wait() afterward
for a synchronous guarantee (e.g. before reusing the port this server
was listening on, or before process exit).  This reactor is
independent of chttpclient's own engine, so destroying the last
chttpsvr has no effect on any chttpclient async work in the same
process.

Also calls clog_close on the server's own logger (the internal
stderr/FATAL-only logger, or the logger derived from the cl passed to
create_chttpsvr_mp / create_chttpsvr).  This never closes a
caller-supplied cl directly, since the server only ever stores a
derived copy of it.

For clean multi-server teardown, call chttpsvr_stop on all servers
first to close their listeners, then call __chttpsvr_destroy on each
handle in any order.

Safe to call on a server that was already force-stopped by
chttpsvr_engine_stop() while still running: that function quiesces
(stops listening, drains in-flight requests, closes connections,
releases the engine reference for) every chttpsvr instance still
registered in the process before the shared reactor is actually torn
down, so calling this function afterward on the same handle only frees
the remaining per-server memory (routers, routes, the server struct
itself); it never double-closes the listener, double-releases the
engine reference, or otherwise repeats work already done.

Also safe to call concurrently with a chttpsvr_engine_stop() that is
still force-stopping this same server on its own background reaper
thread right now (e.g. a signal handler triggering chttpsvr_engine_stop()
while an application thread independently calls this function on the
same handle): whichever of the two finishes tearing srv down first,
the other waits for that teardown to fully complete before proceeding,
rather than racing it.

Does not set the caller's pointer to CHTTPSVR_INVALID; use the
chttpsvr_destroy macro for that.

Safe to call with CHTTPSVR_INVALID (0).
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_destroy (3),
.BR ___chttpsvr_destroy (3),
.BR chttpsvr_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_stop (3),
.BR chttpsvr_engine_wait (3)
